WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.986
Input validation for process execution should support a specific approved
operation.

2
00:00:04.986 --> 00:00:09.702
It should check type, format, length, range, allowed values, and business
intent.

3
00:00:09.702 --> 00:00:13.947
A value that is valid as a display label may not be valid as a file
identifier.

4
00:00:13.947 --> 00:00:17.854
A value that is valid for one processing mode may be unsafe in another.

5
00:00:17.854 --> 00:00:23.514
Validation should be close enough to the operation that reviewers can see
why a value is acceptable.

6
00:00:23.514 --> 00:00:27.422
Allowlists are stronger than attempts to detect every unsafe input.

7
00:00:27.422 --> 00:00:37.865
The application can define approved commands, approved subcommands or
modes, approved file types, approved directories, approved identifiers,
and approved option combinations.

8
00:00:37.865 --> 00:00:41.099
User choices can then be mapped to those internal values.

9
00:00:41.099 --> 00:00:48.847
This design reduces the chance that a user-supplied flag, executable name,
target path, or option changes the meaning of the operation.

10
00:00:48.847 --> 00:00:56.797
Configuration-driven allowlists can help teams manage legitimate
variation, but the configuration itself must be trusted and reviewed.

11
00:00:56.797 --> 00:01:08.116
If an administrator can add a new operation, the approval process should
consider the executable, arguments, permissions, input files, output
files, environment, logging, and rollback behavior.

12
00:01:08.116 --> 00:01:10.475
An allowlist is not just a list of strings.

13
00:01:10.475 --> 00:01:15.326
It is a policy describing which operations the application is prepared to
run safely.

14
00:01:15.326 --> 00:01:19.099
Validation does not replace safe process APIs or least privilege.

15
00:01:19.099 --> 00:01:28.329
A validated argument can still be dangerous if it is placed into shell
syntax, run as an overpowered account, used as an unconstrained path, or
logged with secrets.

16
00:01:28.329 --> 00:01:31.226
Think of validation as one layer in a larger design.

17
00:01:31.226 --> 00:01:42.680
It narrows what the application accepts, while safe APIs, storage
controls, authorization, environment minimization, and privilege
boundaries limit what can happen even when something goes wrong.
